{
  "text": "Your SSD has no moving parts. So where do your files actually live? Inside billions of tiny traps for electrons. Let's open one up and follow a single photo as it gets saved. Every flash chip is a grid of cells. Think of each cell as a tiny bucket with a gate. Push electrons in, and they stay trapped, even with the power off. A full bucket and an empty bucket mean one and zero. But modern drives are greedy. Fill the bucket to four different levels, and one cell holds two bits. Eight levels, three bits. Sixteen levels, four bits. That's QLC, and it's why big drives got so cheap. Then engineers stopped building flat. They stack the cells into towers, over 300 layers high, on a chip smaller than your fingernail. Your photo reaches the controller, the drive's own tiny processor. It splits the photo into pages, spreads them across every chip at once, and writes them in parallel. That's how a fast drive reads over 7,000 megabytes per second. Here's the catch. Every time a bucket is emptied and refilled, its gate wears a little. After one to three thousand rewrites, it starts to leak. So the controller cheats. It never rewrites the same cell. Every new write goes to the least worn cell, and old data gets moved quietly in the background. That's why a typical one terabyte drive is rated for 600 terabytes written. That's over 300 gigabytes every single day for five years. So your photo isn't stored in one place. It's charge, spread across towers of tiny buckets, constantly shuffled to keep your drive alive. Follow for part two: what happens when you hit delete.",
